|~ What is declared under `::string`: the refusal of its
   constructor, and the verbs of strings: the comparisons, which compare
   two strings by their code units in the one order, the verbs that read
   a string, `count`, `take` and `drop` by its characters, and
   `prepend` and `append`. ~|

:string/construct
  |~~ Reads a string as itself. ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:string/PayloadNotStringError
  |~~ `::string` reads its payload as the string it is; a value of
      another kind becomes text by `print`.
  ~~|

:string/PayloadNotStringError/refusesANumber
  |~~ A number payload is refused; `print` makes text of a value. ~~|
  ::law~(::string(1) !| [type /actualType] | eq [::string/PayloadNotStringError ::number])

:string/gt
  |~~ Whether the subject follows the string the call gives. ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:string/gt/ordersByCharacters
  |~~ The string b follows the string a. ~~|
  ::law~("b" | gt "a" | eq true)

:string/gt/than
  |~~ The value the subject is compared with. ~~|
  ~(/0)

:string/gt/than/NotStringError
  |~~ The than is a string. ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::string)

:string/gt/greater
  |~~ Whether the subject comes after the value compared. ~~|
  ::boolean

:string/gte
  |~~ Whether the subject follows or equals the string the call gives. ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:string/gte/holdsOfItself
  |~~ A string follows or equals itself. ~~|
  ::law~("b" | gte "b" | eq true)

:string/gte/than
  |~~ The value the subject is compared with. ~~|
  ~(/0)

:string/gte/than/NotStringError
  |~~ The than is a string. ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::string)

:string/gte/atLeast
  |~~ Whether the subject comes after the value compared or equals it. ~~|
  ::boolean

:string/lt
  |~~ Whether the subject precedes the string the call gives. ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:string/lt/ordersByCharacters
  |~~ The string a precedes the string b. ~~|
  ::law~("a" | lt "b" | eq true)

:string/lt/than
  |~~ The value the subject is compared with. ~~|
  ~(/0)

:string/lt/than/NotStringError
  |~~ The than is a string. ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::string)

:string/lt/less
  |~~ Whether the subject comes before the value compared. ~~|
  ::boolean

:string/lte
  |~~ Whether the subject precedes or equals the string the call gives. ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:string/lte/holdsOfItself
  |~~ A string precedes or equals itself. ~~|
  ::law~("a" | lte "a" | eq true)

:string/lte/than
  |~~ The value the subject is compared with. ~~|
  ~(/0)

:string/lte/than/NotStringError
  |~~ The than is a string. ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::string)

:string/lte/atMost
  |~~ Whether the subject comes before the value compared or equals it. ~~|
  ::boolean

:string/count
  |~~ The number of characters of the string, a letter of any script
      one, the length every other language gives a string.
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:string/count/countsCharacters
  |~~ Three letters count three. ~~|
  ::law~("abc" | count | eq 3)

:string/count/countsALetterOfAnyScriptOne
  |~~ A letter of any script counts one. ~~|
  ::law~("привет" | count | eq 6)

:string/count/countsNoneInTheEmptyString
  |~~ The empty string counts none. ~~|
  ::law~("" | count | eq 0)

:string/count/length
  |~~ How many characters the string holds. ~~|
  ::number

:string/take
  |~~ The first count characters of the string. ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:string/take/takesTheFirstCharacters
  |~~ Two characters of a string are its first two. ~~|
  ::law~("db_host" | take 2 | eq "db")

:string/take/takesNoMoreThanThereAre
  |~~ A count past the end takes the whole string. ~~|
  ::law~("ab" | take 5 | eq "ab")

:string/take/count
  |~~ How many. ~~|
  ~(/0)

:string/take/count/NotNumberError
  |~~ The count is a number. ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::number)

:string/take/count/NotIntegerError
  |~~ The count is a whole number. ~~|
  ::holds~(eq (round))

:string/take/taken
  |~~ The first characters. ~~|
  ::string

:string/drop
  |~~ The string without its first count characters, the rest after a
      prefix of known length.
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:string/drop/dropsAPrefix
  |~~ Dropping a prefix of known length leaves the rest. ~~|
  ::law~("db_host" | drop 3 | eq "host")

:string/drop/count
  |~~ How many. ~~|
  ~(/0)

:string/drop/count/NotNumberError
  |~~ The count is a number. ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::number)

:string/drop/count/NotIntegerError
  |~~ The count is a whole number. ~~|
  ::holds~(eq (round))

:string/drop/rest
  |~~ The characters after the ones dropped. ~~|
  ::string

:string/split
  |~~ The substrings between the occurrences of the separator, as a
      vector. Inverse of `join`.

      The subject of `split` is the string it cuts; a vector of strings
      is cut element by element, `* split ","`.
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:string/split/cutsAtTheSeparator
  |~~ The pieces between the commas. ~~|
  ::law~("a,b,c" | split "," | eq ["a" "b" "c"])

:string/split/cutsAtALineBreak
  |~~ A line break separates as any separator does. ~~|
  ::law~("line1\nline2" | split "\n" | eq ["line1" "line2"])

:string/split/refusesANumber
  |~~ A number is no string to cut. ~~|
  ::law~(42 | split "," !| [type /actualType] | eq [::string/split/SubjectNotStringError ::number])

:string/split/separator
  |~~ The separator the call gives.

      The separator of `split` is a string, `split ","`.
  ~~|
  ~(/0)

:string/split/separator/refusesANumber
  |~~ A number separator is refused. ~~|
  ::law~("abc" | split 42 !| [type /actualType] | eq [::string/split/separator/NotStringError ::number])

:string/split/separator/NotStringError
  |~~ The separator is a string. ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::string)

:string/split/pieces
  |~~ The pieces between the separators, in order. ~~|
  ::vec

:string/lines
  |~~ The lines of a text, as a vector of strings. A `\n` ends a line
      and a `\r` before it belongs to the ending; a newline at the end
      closes the last line rather than opening an empty one, so a text
      reads alike with and without it, and the empty text has no lines.
      The text is then worked on as the vector it has become, and
      `join "\n"` writes it back.

      `lines` breaks a string, the text the command line reads with
      `--raw`; a vector of texts is broken element by element, `* lines`.
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:string/lines/splitsAtLineBreaks
  |~~ Each line is a string. ~~|
  ::law~("north\nsouth" | lines | eq ["north" "south"])

:string/lines/takesACarriageReturnWithItsBreak
  |~~ A carriage return before a break belongs to the ending, and a closing break opens no line. ~~|
  ::law~("one\r\ntwo\n" | lines | eq ["one" "two"])

:string/lines/findsNoLineInTheEmptyString
  |~~ The empty string holds no line. ~~|
  ::law~("" | lines | eq [])

:string/lines/refusesANumber
  |~~ A number is no text. ~~|
  ::law~(42 | lines !| [type /actualType] | eq [::string/lines/SubjectNotStringError ::number])

:string/lines/rows
  |~~ The lines of the text in order, without their breaks. ~~|
  ::vec

:string/lower
  |~~ The string with every letter in lower case, so two strings that
      differ in case alone compare equal once lowered.

      `lower` works on a string; a vector of strings takes it element by
      element, `* lower`, and a field is read first, `/email | lower`.
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:string/lower/lowersEveryLetter
  |~~ Every letter becomes lower case. ~~|
  ::law~("Ann@Example.COM" | lower | eq "ann@example.com")

:string/lower/makesCaseVariantsEqual
  |~~ Two strings that differ in case alone are equal once lowered. ~~|
  ::law~(["Ann" "ANN"] * lower | distinct | count | eq 1)

:string/lower/refusesANumber
  |~~ A number has no letters. ~~|
  ::law~(42 | lower !| [type /actualType] | eq [::string/lower/SubjectNotStringError ::number])

:string/lower/lowercase
  |~~ The text in lower case. ~~|
  ::string

:string/upper
  |~~ The string with every letter in upper case.

      `upper` works on a string; a vector of strings takes it element by
      element, `* upper`.
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:string/upper/raisesEveryLetter
  |~~ Every letter becomes upper case. ~~|
  ::law~("ok, go" | upper | eq "OK, GO")

:string/upper/refusesANumber
  |~~ A number has no letters. ~~|
  ::law~(42 | upper !| [type /actualType] | eq [::string/upper/SubjectNotStringError ::number])

:string/upper/uppercase
  |~~ The text in upper case. ~~|
  ::string

:string/trim
  |~~ The string without the whitespace at its two ends, spaces, tabs
      and line breaks, so a field cut out of a line compares as the text
      it holds.

      `trim` works on a string; a vector of strings takes it element by
      element, `* trim`.
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:string/trim/dropsTheWhitespaceAtTheEnds
  |~~ The spaces at both ends go. ~~|
  ::law~("  db_host 
" | trim | eq "db_host")

:string/trim/cleansTheFieldsOfALine
  |~~ The fields of a line compare as the text they hold once trimmed. ~~|
  ::law~("a, b ,c" | split "," * trim | eq ["a" "b" "c"])

:string/trim/refusesANumber
  |~~ A number has no whitespace to trim. ~~|
  ::law~(42 | trim !| [type /actualType] | eq [::string/trim/SubjectNotStringError ::number])

:string/trim/trimmed
  |~~ The text without the whitespace at its ends. ~~|
  ::string

:string/contains
  |~~ Whether the string holds the needle as a substring; the empty
      needle is always held. Case-sensitive.

      The subject of `::string/contains` is the string searched, where the
      address calls it; the name `contains` reaches a vector and a set by
      their elements.
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:string/contains/findsAWord
  |~~ A word of the string is found. ~~|
  ::law~("hello world" | contains "world" | eq true)

:string/contains/missesAbsentText
  |~~ Text the string lacks is not found. ~~|
  ::law~("hello" | contains "xyz" | eq false)

:string/contains/refusesANumber
  |~~ A number is no string to search. ~~|
  ::law~(42 | string/contains "x" !| [type /actualType] | eq [::string/contains/SubjectNotStringError ::number])

:string/contains/findsTextInside
  |~~ Text inside a word is found. ~~|
  ::law~("hello" | contains "ell" | eq true)

:string/contains/needle
  |~~ The text looked for.

      The needle of `contains` is a string, `contains "x"`; a keyword
      gives its name by `keyword`.
  ~~|
  ~(/0)

:string/contains/needle/refusesANumber
  |~~ A number needle is refused. ~~|
  ::law~("hi" | contains 42 !| [type /actualType] | eq [::string/contains/needle/NotStringError ::number])

:string/contains/needle/NotStringError
  |~~ The needle is a string. ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::string)

:string/contains/found
  |~~ Whether the text holds the part. ~~|
  ::boolean

:string/startsWith
  |~~ Whether the string begins with the prefix; the empty prefix
      always begins it. Case-sensitive.

      The subject of `startsWith` is the string whose beginning is read;
      a field is read first, `/name | startsWith "a"`.
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:string/startsWith/findsThePrefix
  |~~ The string begins with its first word. ~~|
  ::law~("hello world" | startsWith "hello" | eq true)

:string/startsWith/missesAnotherPrefix
  |~~ Another word does not begin it. ~~|
  ::law~("hello" | startsWith "world" | eq false)

:string/startsWith/refusesANumber
  |~~ A number is no string. ~~|
  ::law~(42 | startsWith "x" !| [type /actualType] | eq [::string/startsWith/SubjectNotStringError ::number])

:string/startsWith/prefix
  |~~ The prefix the call gives.

      The prefix of `startsWith` is a string, `startsWith "he"`.
  ~~|
  ~(/0)

:string/startsWith/prefix/refusesANumber
  |~~ A number prefix is refused. ~~|
  ::law~("hi" | startsWith 42 !| [type /actualType] | eq [::string/startsWith/prefix/NotStringError ::number])

:string/startsWith/prefix/NotStringError
  |~~ The prefix is a string. ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::string)

:string/startsWith/starts
  |~~ Whether the text begins with the part. ~~|
  ::boolean

:string/endsWith
  |~~ Whether the string ends with the suffix; the empty suffix always
      ends it. Case-sensitive.

      The subject of `endsWith` is the string whose end is read; a field
      is read first, `/file | endsWith ".json"`.
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:string/endsWith/findsTheSuffix
  |~~ The string ends with its last word. ~~|
  ::law~("hello world" | endsWith "world" | eq true)

:string/endsWith/missesAnotherSuffix
  |~~ Another word does not end it. ~~|
  ::law~("hello" | endsWith "xyz" | eq false)

:string/endsWith/refusesANumber
  |~~ A number is no string. ~~|
  ::law~(42 | endsWith "x" !| [type /actualType] | eq [::string/endsWith/SubjectNotStringError ::number])

:string/endsWith/suffix
  |~~ The suffix the call gives.

      The suffix of `endsWith` is a string, `endsWith ".json"`.
  ~~|
  ~(/0)

:string/endsWith/suffix/refusesANumber
  |~~ A number suffix is refused. ~~|
  ::law~("hi" | endsWith 42 !| [type /actualType] | eq [::string/endsWith/suffix/NotStringError ::number])

:string/endsWith/suffix/NotStringError
  |~~ The suffix is a string. ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::string)

:string/endsWith/ends
  |~~ Whether the text ends with the part. ~~|
  ::boolean

:string/prepend
  |~~ The string with the prefix in front; a tag over the string comes
      back on the answer.
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:string/prepend/putsThePrefixInFront
  |~~ The prefix goes in front. ~~|
  ::law~("world" | prepend "hello " | eq "hello world")

:string/prepend/keepsTheTag
  |~~ A tag over the string comes back on the answer. ~~|
  ::law~(::Box("b") | prepend "a" | eq ::Box("ab"))

:string/prepend/prefix
  |~~ The prefix the call gives.

      The prefix a string takes is a string; a value of another kind
      becomes one by `print`, `prepend (n | print)`.
  ~~|
  ~(/0)

:string/prepend/prefix/refusesANumber
  |~~ A number prefix is refused; `print` makes it text. ~~|
  ::law~("x" | prepend 42 !| [type /actualType] | eq [::string/prepend/prefix/NotStringError ::number])

:string/prepend/prefix/NotStringError
  |~~ The prefix is a string. ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::string)

:string/prepend/prepended
  |~~ The text with the part before it. ~~|
  ::string

:string/append
  |~~ The string with the suffix after it; a tag over the string comes
      back on the answer.
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:string/append/putsTheSuffixAfter
  |~~ The suffix goes after. ~~|
  ::law~("hello" | append " world" | eq "hello world")

:string/append/suffix
  |~~ The suffix the call gives.

      The suffix a string takes is a string; a value of another kind
      becomes one by `print`, `append (n | print)`.
  ~~|
  ~(/0)

:string/append/suffix/refusesANumber
  |~~ A number suffix is refused; `print` makes it text. ~~|
  ::law~("x" | append 42 !| [type /actualType] | eq [::string/append/suffix/NotStringError ::number])

:string/append/suffix/NotStringError
  |~~ The suffix is a string. ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::string)

:string/append/appended
  |~~ The text with the part after it. ~~|
  ::string

:string/parseJson
  |~~ The qlang value a string of plain JSON spells, the reverse of
      `json`: the keys of an object become keywords, an array a vector,
      and scalars pass through. Lossy for the shapes of qlang alone: a
      set arriving from JSON reads as a vector, a keyword as a value as
      a string, and errors have no plain form. It reads input from any
      producer that speaks JSON.

      `parseJson` reads a string of JSON text; a value becomes such text
      by `json`.
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:string/parseJson/readsANumber
  |~~ A JSON number reads as a number. ~~|
  ::law~("42" | parseJson | eq 42)

:string/parseJson/readsAString
  |~~ A JSON string reads as a string. ~~|
  ::law~("\"hello\"" | parseJson | eq "hello")

:string/parseJson/readsAnArrayAsAVector
  |~~ A JSON array reads as a vector. ~~|
  ::law~("[1,2,3]" | parseJson | eq [1 2 3])

:string/parseJson/readsAnObjectAsAMap
  |~~ A JSON object reads as a map of keywords. ~~|
  ::law~("{\"name\":\"alice\"}" | parseJson | eq {:name "alice"})

:string/parseJson/refusesANumber
  |~~ A number is no text to read. ~~|
  ::law~(42 | parseJson !| [type /actualType] | eq [::string/parseJson/SubjectNotStringError ::number])

:string/parseJson/TextNotJsonError
  |~~ `parseJson` could not parse the subject as JSON. The message
      of the host's parser rides on `:message`.
  ~~|

:string/parseJson/TextNotJsonError/refusesBrokenText
  |~~ Text that is no JSON is refused. ~~|
  ::law~("{not json" | parseJson !| type | eq ::string/parseJson/TextNotJsonError)

:string/parseJson/TextNotJsonError/message
  |~~ What the reader of JSON says of the text. ~~|
  ::string

:string/parseJson/NumberNotFiniteError
  |~~ JSON text may write a number past the range of the doubles a
      number of qlang is, `1e400`, which no number of the language
      holds, so reading it is refused rather than turned into an
      infinity. It is met where JSON text is read, by `parseJson` and
      by the command line reading its input, and never in a literal of
      qlang, which the parser refuses itself.
  ~~|

:string/parseJson/NumberNotFiniteError/refusesANumberPastTheRange
  |~~ A number past the range of doubles is refused. ~~|
  ::law~("1e400" | parseJson !| type | eq ::string/parseJson/NumberNotFiniteError)

:string/parseJson/NumberNotFiniteError/path
  |~~ The path to the number in the text. ~~|
  ::vec

:string/keyword
  |~~ The keyword the string names, bridging the world of strings, JSON
      data and text built from parts, with the world of keywords, the
      keys of maps and the discriminators of enums. A keyword answers its
      name by `keyword` again, so the two round-trip.
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:string/keyword/namesAKeyword
  |~~ A string names the keyword of its text. ~~|
  ::law~("foo" | keyword | eq :foo)

:string/keyword/keepsASlash
  |~~ A slash stays in the name. ~~|
  ::law~("qlang/error" | keyword | eq :qlang/error)

:string/keyword/readsBack
  |~~ A keyword's name reads back as the string. ~~|
  ::law~("bar" | keyword | keyword | eq "bar")

:string/keyword/refusesANumberNamingTheVerbs
  |~~ A number has no `keyword`, and the refusal names where it lives. ~~|
  ::law~(42 | keyword !| /addresses | eq #[::keyword/keyword ::string/keyword])

:string/keyword/key
  |~~ The keyword the text names. ~~|
  ::keyword

:string/parse
  |~~ The quote of the steps the source text spells, the reverse of
      `parse` on a quote. Pair it with `apply /` to evaluate source
      text without leaving qlang. A source the parser refuses answers a
      `::quote/ParseError` on the fail-track, which `!|` reads like every
      other error.
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:string/parse/readsTheSteps
  |~~ The source reads as its steps. ~~|
  ::law~("1 | add 1" | parse | count | eq 2)

:string/parse/readsTheQuote
  |~~ The source reads as the quote it spells. ~~|
  ::law~("add 1 2" | parse | eq ~(add 1 2))

:string/parse/refusesANumberNamingTheVerbs
  |~~ A number has no `parse`, and the refusal names where it lives. ~~|
  ::law~(42 | parse !| /addresses | eq #[::quote/parse ::string/parse])
